 {'idx': 6, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'group Spin(R) these become isomorphic, so the spin group has a complex spinor representation of dimension 2. More generally, spinor groups and pin groups over any field have similar representations whose exact structure depends on the structure of the corresponding Clifford algebras: whenever a Clifford algebra has a factor that is a matrix algebra over some division algebra, we get a corresponding representation of the pin and spin groups over that division algebra. For examples over the reals see the article on spinors. To describe the real spin representations, one must know how the spin group sits inside its Clifford', 'title': 'Clifford algebra'}
