 {'idx': 3, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': "the long diagonal may be used to change direction, and is especially helpful in a large lesson, to get the group to change direction with little risk that they will run into each other. More importantly, it is a good training tool to test the horse's straightness. Certain movements, such as flying changes, are sometimes taught across the diagonal, as are lengthening of stride. This figure appears in most dressage tests. The rider goes from one K,H,M, or F to the opposite long side, but aims for either E or B. This can also be performed the opposite direction, for", 'title': 'Riding figures'}
