 {'idx': 11, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'cover "C" of "K" a finite subcover. If a set is closed and bounded, then it is compact. If a set "S" in R is bounded, then it can be enclosed within an "n"-box where "a" > 0. By the property above, it is enough to show that "T" is compact. Assume, by way of contradiction, that "T" is not compact. Then there exists an infinite open cover "C" of "T" that does not admit any finite subcover. Through bisection of each of the sides of "T", the box "T" can be broken up into 2 sub "n"-boxes, each of', 'title': 'Heine–Borel theorem'}
