[{'idx': 0, 'is_supporting': True, 'paragraph_text': 'Cain and Abel In the biblical Book of Genesis, Cain and Abel are the first two sons of Adam and Eve. Cain, the firstborn, was a farmer, and his brother Abel was a shepherd. The brothers made sacrifices to God, each of his own produce, but God favored Abel\'s sacrifice instead of Cain\'s. Cain then murdered Abel, whereupon God punished Cain to a life of wandering. Cain then dwelt in the land of Nod (, "wandering"), where he built a city and fathered the line of descendants beginning with Enoch. The narrative never explicitly states Cain\'s motive for murdering his', 'title': 'Cain and Abel'}
