 {'idx': 2, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'schools of Hinduism, the concept of self (atman) is the real on-looker, personal agent and cognizer. The Pali Abdhidhamma and Theravada Buddhism considered all existence as "dhamma", and left the ontological questions about reality and the nature of "dhamma" unexplained. According to Renard, Advaita\'s theory of three levels of reality is built on the two levels of reality found in the Madhyamika. A central concern for Shankara, in his objections against Buddhism, is what he perceives as nihilism of the Buddhists. Shankara states that there "must be something beyond cognition, namely a cognizer," which he asserts is the self-evident "Atman"', 'title': 'Advaita Vedanta'}
