 {'idx': 5, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Pauline Irene Nguene Pauline Irene Nguene, was born in Kendeck, is a Cameroonian politician, Minister of Social Affairs in the government of Philemon Yang. Nguene is from Minka village (Makak) in the Nyong-et-Kellé division in the Central Region (Cameroon). Nguene graduated in Petrochemicals in the United States. Subsequently, she completed this training in Canada with an MBA. On January 13, 2009, Nguene was appointed head of the Support Board for the Completion of Partnership Contracts (CARPA). It is an organization charged with promoting the economic partnership between the public and private sectors. She is also Chair of the board of', 'title': 'Pauline Irene Nguene'}
 {'idx': 6, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'edited by Karl Kroeger. 144 pages, . Abraham Wood (composer) Abraham Wood (1752 –1804) was one of the first American composers. Wood was born in Massachusetts Bay Colony and was a drummer during the American Revolutionary War. He wrote "Warren" to commemorate the army officer Joseph Warren (1741–1775), who died courageously in the Battle of Bunker Hill and he wrote "A Hymn on Peace" to commemorate the Treaty of Paris that officially ended the Revolutionary War. This work was circulated as single pamphlet instead of part of a larger collection of sacred pieces, which was more common of the time.', 'title': 'Abraham Wood (composer)'}
