 {'idx': 11, 'is_supporting': False, 'paragraph_text': 'Nabíl-i-Akbar Áqá Muḥammad-i-Qá\'iní (also known as Fadil-i-Qa\'ini ("Learned One of Qa\'ín") and surnamed Nabíl-i-Akbar ();\u200e 1829–1892) was a distinguished Bahá\'í from the town of Qá\'in. He is one of 19 Apostles of Bahá\'u\'lláh, and referred to by `Abdu\'l-Bahá as a Hand of the Cause of God. In the abjad notation, the name "Muhammad" has the same numerical value as "Nabíl". Nabíl-i-Akbar was the recipient of a tablet from Bahá\'u\'lláh, the Tablet of Wisdom. \'Abdu\'l-Bahá recounted: Nabíl completed his studentship under Shaykh Murtaday-i-Ansari, and after he had obtained his sanction and blessing, Nabíl moved from Najaf to Baghdad. This teacher was', 'title': 'Nabíl-i-Akbar'}
